Goals:
-----

 - Develop a treatment of CPU and GPU versions of the same code that:
 
     1. Allows maintenance of a code base, with a single definition of 
     each class that can be used on both CPU and GPU, whenever possible.

     2. Avoiding the need to create parallel sets of classes (or trivial 
     subclasses) in the Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

     3. Makes efficient use of existing code when possible - avoid the
     need to start over.

     4. Create a manageable build system that allows for maintaining 
     object files for several compiled versions of the code. Ideally, 
     I'd like to be able to maintain several versions simultaneously.

     5. Create a manageable system for unit tests, with little or no
     code duplication in the tests.

Comments: Goal 1 has been largely accomplished b current system of class
templates in namespace Rp, using "Types" classes as template parameters
that encapsulate collections of type aliases.  This file proposes a way 
to accomplish goal 2, and avoid the need for distinct Rpc and Rpg 
namespaces.  

Discussion
----------

The most basic problem in maintaining CPU and GPU versions of the same
code arises from the need to create CPU and GPU versions of the classes
that store fields that are defined on the nodes of a mesh.  All or almost 
all of the arrays that are stored on the GPU in the current version are
fields of this type.  Relevant mathematical operations include things 
like binary and in-place (compound) arithmetic operations, exponentiation, 
and FFTs.

A strategy for specializing behavior for CPUs and GPU-enabled code 
could differentiate behavior either at run time (via inheritance and 
polymorphic virtual functions) or at compile time (via templates).

A. Run time polymorphism via inheritance and virtual functions:

Strategy: Create an interface / base class for each field class, and define
subclasses designed to use CPU and GPU memory.  Run-time polymorphism must 
rely on the use of virtual functions to distinguish behavior, and so requires 
that all mathematical operation that are performed on a field must be 
implemented by virtual functions declared by a base field class.

Comments:

   - In virtual functions that implement operations for which the output
     is a field, and one or more other fields may be provided as input
     parameters, it is natural to call the member function on the field 
     that will hold the output, since there is always one output, but
     there may be any number of inputs.

   - To create generic code, functions that take fields as parameters
     must take references to a base class as parameters. Operations are 
     actually only allowed for input and output fields that are defined
     on the same grid and that use the same "backend".  There thus must 
     be a run-time check of some sort of label to ensure that all fields 
     involved in an operation are compatible in this sense. Because both
     mesh dimensions and backend must be compatible, it is natural to 
     create a "layout" structure that encapsulates these two attributes 
     and checks them together. 

B. Compile time polymorphism via templates.

This is the proposal that is outlined in the remainder of this file. In 
this approach, we define class templates that take a template parameter 
that uniquely identifies the backend that is in use.

Specifically, to take advantage of analogies with the current design of 
class templates in namespace Pscf::Rp, I propose to define a set of 
classes that each take two template parameters: 

   - The integer dimension of space D=1,2, or 3, and
   - A class parameter T that is uniquely associated with a particular
     backend.

Use of a class to identify the backend (rather than some simpler form
of identifier such as a enum or string) allows the class to contain
additional information, such as associated types used by the related 
FFT library). This is also very similar to the existing system, in 
which each class template in namespace Rp takes D and a "Types" class 
as template parameters.

The field RField that represents real data on a periodic mesh would
thus become a template <int D, int T> class RField. If we define a
class BC to represent a Cpu backend and BU to represent a GPU backend
programmed in CUDA, the RField<3,BU> would be a field defined on a 3D
mesh and allocated in global GPU device memory.

Class templates with these two template parameters would be defined both
for basic Field and FFT classes, and for a variety of higher level 
classes that directly or indirectly own such fields.  A class template 
Parent<D,T> for a parent class that owns an RField can then have a member 
RField<D,T>. In this manner we can create a hierarchy of templates that 
own each other, such that all of the templates used in a given executable 
would have the same values for both D and T.

Comments:

  - Mathematical operations can be implemented as overloaded free 
    functions (for vector operations and reduction operations) or as 
    member functions of an associated FFT<D,T> class for FFT operations, 
    exactly as done currently. It appears that no changes would need 
    to be made to any of the existing mathematical functions. Continued
    use of global functions for vector operationsand reduction operations
    makes it convenient to add new mathematical operations when useful 
    without cluttering up the interface of the field classes with a 
    growing list of virtual functions.

  - The check that all fields or arrays involved in a mathematical 
    operation must use the same backend would be automatically imposed 
    by the C++ type system, rather than as run-time-checks: The compiler 
    can choose the correct version of overloaded functions for use in
    CPU or GPU code based on the types of the function parameters.
    
  - I need to think about how to implement unit tests, but it may
    be natural to re-define these as member function templates that 
    take the backend types class T as a template argument. The
    unification of unit tests is not fully fleshed out.

Thoughts:

  - The template approach is arguably a more natural conceptual fit to 
    the way the software is used: The intent is that a single backend 
    will always be used throughout execution of a given program, so 
    the backend for the entire program can be specified at compile time, 
    as a template parameter.  The run-time flexibility promised by a 
    system based on run-time polymorphism simply isn't needed, or ever 
    used. 

  - I think that the strongest argument for preferring compile-time 
    polymorhpisms is a result of the strengths and weaknesses of the  
    C++ language: The C++ mechanisms for working with templates in a 
    large code base are clumsier than mechanisms for working with a 
    system based on inheritance. The awkwardness of C++ mechanisms for 
    working with templates is largely a result of the history of the 
    language as an extension of C, the continued use of a text 
    preprocessor as the way of importing interface information, and 
    the way that this old (archaic?) mechanism form importing modules
    interacts with C++ templates.  The added complexity required to 
    work with templates in C++ is, however, a real and unavoidable 
    consideration. It provides a sensible reason to consider a 
    solution based on run-time polymorphism even for problems in 
    compile-time polymorphism is an appropriate option.

  - PSCF already treats the dimension of space D as a template parameter.
    The decision to do this was made during development of code involving 
    crystallography and space group symmetry, and was based on a desire 
    to be able to define small vectors and matrices with dimensions 
    given by the dimension of space on the stack, for reasons of 
    convenience and efficiency, and to avoid the need to have the dimension
    of space stored by every class and every object.  In retrospect, the 
    focus on efficiency may have been misplaced, since operations that 
    involve these sorts of small spatial vectors and matrices are never
    a significant fraction of total computation time.  This decision 
    ended up infecting all classes designed specifically for use with
    periodic systems, yielding a design relies heavily on templates. 

  - The current version of PSCF uses a system of templates that is 
    in some ways very similar to that proposed here. In the current
    system, the Rp namespace already contains a set of classes that
    almost all have two class template templates analogous to the 
    two proposed here.  In the process of developing the current 
    version, we have already developed methods to avoid some of the 
    potential problems of large-template based frameworks in C++ (e.g., 
    potentially long compilation times and mysterious compiler error 
    messages) by explicitly instantiating template specializations 
    of each class for all allowed values of D=1,2, and 3. 

  - In the current code, for each class named "Class", there is
    generally a class template Rp::Class<D,T> that defines all or most 
    of the class definition, and subclasses Rpc::Class<D> and
    Rpg::Class<D> that are derived from specializations of Rp::Class
    with different values for the Types class. The subclasses defined
    in Rpc and Rpg are often trivial, with little or no new code, 
    and are required simply in order to segregate names for Cpu and 
    Gpu code into different namespaces. In the new system, we would 
    work directly with specializations of Rp::Class<D,T> with different 
    values for the types class T= BC or T=BU. The new design would thus
    remove the need for the Rpc and Rpg namespaces.

  - By getting rid of the Rpc and Rpg namespaces, we would get rid of
    the need for the Types classes to maintain sets of aliases that
    define generic names that refer to classes defined in the Rpc and
    Rpg namespaces. The Types classes definitions would become much
    smaller, and would contain only a few types definitions for types
    used by the FFT classes.

  - In a system in which most classes take two template parameters
    (dimension of space D, and a second parameter T that acts as an
    identifier for a backend, I can see two reasonable options for 
    the nature of the second template parameter:

      A. It can be a class, as described above. One advantage of 
      this is that this provides a simple syntax for accessing
      associated information about associated types or other info, 
      via "typename T::Complex" or "T:: backend", etc. A class can
      also define a few static functions to do things like initialize
      or clean up an infrastructure used by a particular backend.

      B. A possible alternative is to make the second parameter an 
      enum value (enum class Backend) and then use Traits classes to 
      to access related information. 

    Either would work fine. One main reason that I curently prefer
    A (use of a class) is that it is more similar to the current 
    system and provides an easier path for conversion, as discussed 
    below.

  - As already noted, the proposed template based proposal would allow 
    re-use of all of the existing code for basic mathematical operations, 
    without modification. As discussed below, it also provides a path 
    to an improved design by stepwise changes, without ever breaking 
    the code. It appears to me that a change to an inheritance based
    design would require a more drastic rewrite.

  - The template-based system proposed here might or might not have
    been the best option if we were designing a new package from
    scratch. I would see an argument for the inheritance based system 
    if I were starting over, because I think it would be somewhat easier 
    for grad student developers to understand.  The proposed 
    template-based design is a reasonable alternative that requires
    less rewriting of the current design. 

